# Linux export map for libese.so — the equivalent of esent.def on Windows.
#
# Only the public Jet* API is exported. Every engine internal, OS-layer
# entry point, and C runtime shim is made local. Localizing the shims is
# load-bearing: winapi_crt.cxx defines wcslen/wcscpy/wcscmp/... built with
# -fshort-wchar (2-byte wchar_t). Those names collide with glibc's 4-byte
# versions, and glibc is always earlier in the process's global symbol
# scope, so when libese.so is dlopen'd its own intra-library calls would
# otherwise bind to glibc's 4-byte routines and corrupt every wide string.
# Marking them local keeps libese's references bound to libese's own
# definitions and removes them from .dynsym so nothing can interpose them.
# GetLastError/SetLastError are exported so libese's thread-local last-error
# slot interposes libnls's own copy (libnls is a DT_NEEDED child, later in
# the load order).  Without this the two libraries keep separate slots: a
# SetLastError inside libnls (e.g. ERROR_INSUFFICIENT_BUFFER from LCMapStringEx
# on a long sort key) is invisible to the engine's GetLastError, which then
# misreads the failure as JET_errUnicodeTranslationFail.  Unlike the wcs*
# shims, exporting these is safe: glibc defines no *LastError to collide with.
ESE {
    global:
        Jet*;
        GetLastError;
        SetLastError;
    local:
        *;
};
